<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frankenthal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frankenthal is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between Worms and Ludwigshafen. In 1562 Elector Frederick III settled Calvinist refugees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> there, and their community of Flemish and Walloon craftsmen made it a centre of tapestry weaving and, later, of a celebrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> manufactory (1755–1800). It was fortified as part of the Electoral Palatinate's defences in the early seventeenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frankenthal.jpg" alt="Frankenthal" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mundartpoet at German Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2006-06-09-1145-FT-Rathaus01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Frankenthal was first mentioned in 772. An Augustinian monastery was built there in 1119, and its ruins, known after the founder as the Erkenbertruine, still stand in the town centre. In the second half of the 16th century people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, persecuted for their religious beliefs, settled in the town. They were industrious and artistic, brought economic prosperity and included important carpet weavers, jewellers and artists, whose Frankenthaler Malerschule, or Frankenthal school of painting, acquired some fame. In 1577 Count Palatine Johann Casimir raised the settlement to the status of a city.</p><p>In 1600 Frankenthal was converted into a fortress. In 1621 English soldiers under Sir Horace Vere garrisoned it and the Spanish besieged it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, after which troops of the opposing sides occupied it in turn. Trade and industry were ruined, and the town was not rebuilt until 1682. French troops burnt it to the ground in 1689 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">War of the Grand Alliance</a>, and it did not fully recover for more than fifty years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1750 Elector Charles Theodore made Frankenthal a centre of industry. A port was built between 1772 and 1781, factories were opened and mulberry trees were planted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> production. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> factory opened in 1755 and remained in production until 1800. The town came under French occupation in 1797 and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1816, and modern industrialisation began around 1859. The synagogue built in 1884 was burnt to the ground on Kristallnacht in 1938, and a bombing raid in 1943 almost destroyed the town centre. Since 1946 Frankenthal has been part of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, and it had 49,051 inhabitants in 2022 and some medium-sized industries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frankenthal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Palatinate welcomed Calvinist refugees from France and the Netherlands, who founded settlements such as <b>Frankenthal</b>, and Frederick IV founded Mannheim as a fortified town in 1606.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
